Ladybird measures a percentage-height table cell after laying out
its descendants against the resolved height. It aligns that measured
content within the final row height, while keeping the percentage
basis for descendants and the final containing block for positioned
children. This also keeps auto-height tables on their own sizing path.

Blink separates the cell fragment size from the unconstrained content
size in FinalizeTableCellLayout(), and uses their difference to move
the children for middle or bottom alignment. Gecko does the same in
nsTableCellFrame::AlignChildWithinCell(): It aligns the inner frame
within the cell, keeping that content frame separate from the cell.
WebKit uses RenderTableCell::computeIntrinsicPadding() to distribute
the space between the row height and the cell before stretching it.
These paths all align the content rather than treating the specified
cell height as content that fills the alignment space.

The saved minecraft.wiki viewer sets height:100% on both its table
and its cell. Chrome centers its 64px image at y=375 in the 814px
image area. The regression also checks percentage-height descendants
and positioned children, whose percentage bases must remain intact.
